# User Development Preferences

Apply on every task unless the user overrides in the current message.

## How to work

- **Think before acting**: Read full files; trace dependencies across domains (auth, contracts, payments, reviews, Nostr). Do not trust stale docs or comments—verify in source.
- **High confidence before done**: Investigate until types, lint, and tests agree (~95% confidence). Surface uncertainty instead of guessing.
- **Minimal scope**: Smallest correct diff; no drive-by refactors, no extra files unless necessary.
- **Tests after fixes**: Run targeted tests for the touched area first; full `test:unit` / `test:ci` when the change is broad or before calling work complete.
- **Iterate until green**: If CI or `test:unit` fails, fix root cause and re-run; do not stop at partial fixes.

## Nostr relay probes

- Never publish test or probe events of kind 31000 or 30402 to `wss://relay1.bittasker.com` or `wss://relay2.bittasker.com`. Those kinds are the live task and service listings. A probe there shows up on production `/tasks`.
- For a relay write check, use a non-listing kind (kind 1 with a `t=bittasker-probe` tag) or a local or test relay. Do not use relay1-dev or relay2-dev for listing kinds either when the goal is only to see whether EVENT is accepted.
- Delete a probe you published with a NIP-09 kind 5 from the same key, including the `e` id and the addressable `a` coordinate. Do not delete events whose secret key you do not have.

## Naming and APIs

- **Function names: at most three words** (e.g. `fetchProfileMetadata`, `parseProfileEvents`, `mergeProfileEvent`).
- **No redundant suffixes** (`Display`, `Ndk`, `Helper`) when the module or type already conveys context.
- **No deprecated wrappers**: migrate call sites, then delete old exports—do not leave `@deprecated` shims indefinitely.
- **Reuse existing code**: search before creating; extend with props/composition instead of duplicating components.
- **Match local conventions**: naming, imports, error handling, and comment density of the file you edit.

## Code quality

- **TypeScript**: proper types; `unknown` + guards instead of `any`; no `eslint-disable` / `ts-ignore` / `ts-expect-error`.
- **Svelte 5**: `$state`, `$derived`, `$props`, `onclick`—no legacy `on:`, `export let`, or `createEventDispatcher`.
- **Functional style**: pure business logic; immutable data; early returns; side effects in stores/services only.
- **No `void` on promises**: use `async` flows with `.catch()` or explicit handling at the source.
- **Platform**: guard Capacitor plugins; wrap browser-only APIs in `if (browser)` where the app may SSR/prerender.
- **Security**: Web Crypto for crypto; never log keys/seeds; validate at boundaries; safe error messages.

## Stack defaults (BitTasker)

- **UI**: DaisyUI + Tailwind; semantic colors; `alert-outline`; mobile-first; no inline hex.
- **i18n**: every user-facing string via `$t()`; en + es; run `python3 scripts/find-missing-translations.py --fix-suggestions` before commit when locales change.
- **Nostr I/O**: NDK under `src/lib/nostr/` (profile module, `direct-messages.ts`); avoid resurrecting deleted `nostr-tools` pool monoliths.
- **Pubkey lookup**: `normalizeProfileLookupKey()` → `.hexPubkey` / `.cacheKey`.

## Testing

- Meaningful tests only—behavior and edge cases, not obvious tautologies.
- Mock at boundaries (NDK, Capacitor, APIs); update mocks when renaming public exports.
- `npm run check` after TypeScript changes; `npm run lint` before finish.

## Git and delivery

- **Commit only when the user asks**; new commits (no amend unless hooks require it); never `--no-verify`.
- **Do not add markdown docs** unless requested.
- **After large features** (multi-file, new subsystem, or user-visible flow): end the implementation reply with a **suggested commit message** (1–2 sentences, focus on why) and a short **test plan** checklist—even if the user has not asked to commit yet.

## What to avoid

- Over-engineering, 3-line re-export files, and compatibility aliases without a migration plan.
- Long identifier names and duplicate helpers for the same concept.
- Assuming the first solution is correct without running checks/tests.
- Proactive commits or scope creep beyond the request.
